request.user_prompt:
You are an expert HPC compiler optimizing C code for CPU execution
with OpenMP. Prioritize loop transformations that improve locality and
parallelism, then apply suitable OpenMP directives and clauses.

Task and scope:
- Optimize only code in regions delimited by '#pragma scope' and
  '#pragma endscop'. Preserve these marker lines unchanged.
- Preserve function signatures, program inputs/outputs, and original semantics.
- You MAY restructure loops and adjust associated C statements within the
  marked regions when needed for the priority loop transformations below.
- Do not modify code outside the marked regions. Do not add dependencies,
  data races, invalid OpenMP directives, or unsupported transformations.

HIGHEST PRIORITY — LOOP TRANSFORMATIONS (examine FIRST, in this order):
1. Loop tiling / blocking: investigate tiling of suitable nested loops to improve
   cache locality, data reuse, and parallel granularity. Choose legal tile sizes.
2. Loop fusion: investigate merging adjacent loops with compatible iteration
   spaces when no dependence makes the fused loop incorrect.
3. Loop skewing: investigate skewing loops with carried dependencies where an
   affine change can expose legal parallelism.

IMPORTANT: These three are loop transformations, not standalone OpenMP pragmas.
Do NOT falsely claim '#pragma omp' alone performs them.

SECOND PRIORITY — OPENMP DIRECTIVE-LEVEL OPTIMIZATIONS:
4. Parallelism: 'parallel for', 'for', 'collapse(n)'.
5. Scheduling: 'schedule(static|dynamic|guided|runtime)'.
6. Synchronization: 'nowait' only when safe; 'atomic'/'critical' when needed.
7. Data sharing: 'private', 'firstprivate', 'lastprivate', 'shared', 'default(none)'.
8. Reductions: 'reduction(+:x)' etc. Consider FP reassociation.
9. SIMD: 'simd', 'parallel for simd', 'simdlen(n)', 'safelen(n)', 'aligned(...)'.
10. Thread management: 'num_threads(n)', 'proc_bind(close/spread/primary)'.

Optimization decision process:
- FIRST examine tiling, fusion, and skewing, in that priority order.
- THEN examine all seven OpenMP categories, selecting only beneficial, legal choices.
- Maintain correct dependence order, variable scope, data sharing, synchronization.
- Do not change observable behavior. If no safe optimization is available, return
  the original code unchanged.
- Keep all transformations strictly within the marked scope regions.

Original code:
/**
 * This version is stamped on May 10, 2016
 *
 * Contact:
 *   Louis-Noel Pouchet <pouchet.ohio-state.edu>
 *   Tomofumi Yuki <tomofumi.yuki.fr>
 *
 * Web address: http://polybench.sourceforge.net
 */
/* 3mm.c: this file is part of PolyBench/C */

#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <math.h>

/* Include polybench common header. */
#include <polybench.h>

/* Include benchmark-specific header. */
#include "3mm.h"


/* Array initialization. */
static
void init_array(int ni, int nj, int nk, int nl, int nm,
		DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(A,NI,NK,ni,nk),
		DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(B,NK,NJ,nk,nj),
		DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(C,NJ,NM,nj,nm),
		DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(D,NM,NL,nm,nl))
{
  int i, j;

  for (i = 0; i < ni; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < nk; j++)
      A[i][j] = (DATA_TYPE) ((i*j+1) % ni) / (5*ni);
  for (i = 0; i < nk; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < nj; j++)
      B[i][j] = (DATA_TYPE) ((i*(j+1)+2) % nj) / (5*nj);
  for (i = 0; i < nj; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < nm; j++)
      C[i][j] = (DATA_TYPE) (i*(j+3) % nl) / (5*nl);
  for (i = 0; i < nm; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < nl; j++)
      D[i][j] = (DATA_TYPE) ((i*(j+2)+2) % nk) / (5*nk);
}


/* DCE code. Must scan the entire live-out data.
   Can be used also to check the correctness of the output. */
static
void print_array(int ni, int nl,
		 DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(G,NI,NL,ni,nl))
{
  int i, j;

  POLYBENCH_DUMP_START;
  POLYBENCH_DUMP_BEGIN("G");
  for (i = 0; i < ni; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < nl; j++) {
	if ((i * ni + j) % 20 == 0) fprintf (POLYBENCH_DUMP_TARGET, "\n");
	fprintf (POLYBENCH_DUMP_TARGET, DATA_PRINTF_MODIFIER, G[i][j]);
    }
  POLYBENCH_DUMP_END("G");
  POLYBENCH_DUMP_FINISH;
}


/* Main computational kernel. The whole function will be timed,
   including the call and return. */
static
void kernel_3mm(int ni, int nj, int nk, int nl, int nm,
		DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(E,NI,NJ,ni,nj),
		DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(A,NI,NK,ni,nk),
		DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(B,NK,NJ,nk,nj),
		DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(F,NJ,NL,nj,nl),
		DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(C,NJ,NM,nj,nm),
		DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(D,NM,NL,nm,nl),
		DATA_TYPE POLYBENCH_2D(G,NI,NL,ni,nl))
{
  int i, j, k;

#pragma scop
  /* E := A*B */
  for (i = 0; i < _PB_NI; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < _PB_NJ; j++)
      {
	E[i][j] = SCALAR_VAL(0.0);
	for (k = 0; k < _PB_NK; ++k)
	  E[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j];
      }
  /* F := C*D */
  for (i = 0; i < _PB_NJ; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < _PB_NL; j++)
      {
	F[i][j] = SCALAR_VAL(0.0);
	for (k = 0; k < _PB_NM; ++k)
	  F[i][j] += C[i][k] * D[k][j];
      }
  /* G := E*F */
  for (i = 0; i < _PB_NI; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < _PB_NL; j++)
      {
	G[i][j] = SCALAR_VAL(0.0);
	for (k = 0; k < _PB_NJ; ++k)
	  G[i][j] += E[i][k] * F[k][j];
      }
#pragma endscop

}


int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
  /* Retrieve problem size. */
  int ni = NI;
  int nj = NJ;
  int nk = NK;
  int nl = NL;
  int nm = NM;

  /* Variable declaration/allocation. */
  POLYBENCH_2D_ARRAY_DECL(E, DATA_TYPE, NI, NJ, ni, nj);
  POLYBENCH_2D_ARRAY_DECL(A, DATA_TYPE, NI, NK, ni, nk);
  POLYBENCH_2D_ARRAY_DECL(B, DATA_TYPE, NK, NJ, nk, nj);
  POLYBENCH_2D_ARRAY_DECL(F, DATA_TYPE, NJ, NL, nj, nl);
  POLYBENCH_2D_ARRAY_DECL(C, DATA_TYPE, NJ, NM, nj, nm);
  POLYBENCH_2D_ARRAY_DECL(D, DATA_TYPE, NM, NL, nm, nl);
  POLYBENCH_2D_ARRAY_DECL(G, DATA_TYPE, NI, NL, ni, nl);

  /* Initialize array(s). */
  init_array (ni, nj, nk, nl, nm,
	      POLYBENCH_ARRAY(A),
	      POLYBENCH_ARRAY(B),
	      POLYBENCH_ARRAY(C),
	      POLYBENCH_ARRAY(D));

  /* Start timer. */
  polybench_start_instruments;

  /* Run kernel. */
  kernel_3mm (ni, nj, nk, nl, nm,
	      POLYBENCH_ARRAY(E),
	      POLYBENCH_ARRAY(A),
	      POLYBENCH_ARRAY(B),
	      POLYBENCH_ARRAY(F),
	      POLYBENCH_ARRAY(C),
	      POLYBENCH_ARRAY(D),
	      POLYBENCH_ARRAY(G));

  /* Stop and print timer. */
  polybench_stop_instruments;
  polybench_print_instruments;

  /* Prevent dead-code elimination. All live-out data must be printed
     by the function call in argument. */
  polybench_prevent_dce(print_array(ni, nl,  POLYBENCH_ARRAY(G)));

  /* Be clean. */
  POLYBENCH_FREE_ARRAY(E);
  POLYBENCH_FREE_ARRAY(A);
  POLYBENCH_FREE_ARRAY(B);
  POLYBENCH_FREE_ARRAY(F);
  POLYBENCH_FREE_ARRAY(C);
  POLYBENCH_FREE_ARRAY(D);
  POLYBENCH_FREE_ARRAY(G);

  return 0;
}


Expert Knowledge (use when consistent with correctness and scope):
Expert Knowledge Documentation: PolyBench 2mm
Static optimization analysis of PPCG, PLUTO, Gemini, and GPT-5.2 outputs
Scope. This document analyzes the supplied 2mm implementations qualitatively for likely speedup behavior. It compares the generated loop structure, OpenMP strategy, tiling, vectorization, locality, and overhead. It does not report measured runtime speedups; performance conclusions should be validated on the target hardware.
1. Application Characteristics
2mm computes two consecutive dense matrix multiplications: tmp = α·A·B, followed by D = β·D + tmp·C. The kernel is compute intensive and has substantial reuse potential, so performance is strongly affected by cache locality, parallel work distribution, vectorization, and the overhead of entering OpenMP regions.
Most useful parallelism: independent output rows/cells of tmp and D.
Most useful locality optimization: block/tile the matrix-multiplication loops so A/tmp blocks and portions of B/C remain cache-resident.
Most useful low-level optimization: SIMD/vectorization of contiguous inner loops when legal and profitable.
Key risk: a transformation can increase loop-control complexity or scheduling overhead enough to offset locality gains, especially for small problem sizes.
2. Supplied 2mm Implementations
Approach	Files inspected	Main transformation observed
PPCG	2mm_ppcg_openmp.c
2mm_ppcg_tile.c	Outer-loop OpenMP; tiled variant uses 32-sized blocking in i/j/k dimensions.
PLUTO	2mm.pluto.c
2mm.pluto.tile.c	Affine/polyhedral scheduling with OpenMP and vector directives; tiled variant adds 32-sized blocking and more aggressive restructuring.
Gemini	gemini/.../2mm/2mm.c	Two separate OpenMP parallel-for regions over the outer i loop; original loop order otherwise largely preserved.
GPT-5.2	gpt-5.2/.../2mm/2mm.c	One OpenMP parallel region containing two collapse(2), static-scheduled work-sharing loops.
3. Approach-by-Approach Expert Analysis
3.1 PPCG
Observed optimization. The OpenMP version parallelizes the outer i dimension separately for the two matrix-multiplication phases. The tiled version keeps OpenMP parallelism but introduces 32×32-style blocking across the output and reduction dimensions, with boundary handling through min/max expressions.
Advantages for speedup
Both phases expose coarse-grained row/block parallelism with no reduction synchronization between threads because each thread updates disjoint output elements.
The tiled version can substantially improve temporal and spatial cache locality by reusing blocks of A, B, tmp, and C before moving to the next tile.
Blocking the reduction dimension can reduce cache misses and memory-bandwidth pressure, which is especially valuable for dense matrix multiplication.
The transformation is systematic and dependence-aware, making it suitable as a reliable baseline for legal loop restructuring.
Disadvantages / risks
The untiled OpenMP version leaves the original memory-access pattern mostly intact, so B and C accesses can still suffer poor cache locality as matrices grow.
The tiled version fixes the tile size at 32; this may be suboptimal for a particular cache hierarchy, data type, thread count, or matrix size.
Boundary min/max logic and deeper loop nesting add control overhead. For small matrices, this overhead can offset tiling benefits.
Two separate OpenMP parallel-for regions introduce fork/join overhead between the tmp and D phases.
Expert knowledge. For 2mm, PPCG’s tiled output is generally the more promising performance template than its simple OpenMP output when matrices are large enough to benefit from cache blocking. The most reusable idea is to combine coarse thread-level parallelism with cache-sized blocking, while tuning the tile size rather than assuming 32 is universally optimal.
3.2 PLUTO
Observed optimization. The non-tiled output applies polyhedral rescheduling, OpenMP parallel loops, and explicit vectorization directives. It also restructures initialization and computation. The tiled output adds 32-sized tiles and fuses/reorders several operations into a more complex generated schedule.
Advantages for speedup
PLUTO can exploit more transformation freedom than simple pragma insertion, including loop fusion, reordering, parallelization, and vector-friendly loop formation.
Explicit ivdep/vector directives can help the compiler generate SIMD code for contiguous inner loops, increasing arithmetic throughput.
The tiled version improves locality across the two dense multiplication stages and exposes cache-blocked computation similar to optimized GEMM structure.
Fusing initialization/scaling with surrounding transformed loops may reduce extra full-array passes and improve cache reuse in favorable cases.
Disadvantages / risks
The generated control structure is much more complex, with many bounds, conditionals, and transformed induction variables; this increases instruction overhead and makes performance sensitive to problem dimensions.
Aggressive fusion/reordering can create less intuitive access patterns and may not always map cleanly to the target cache/microarchitecture despite being dependence-correct.
The tiled output again uses a fixed tile size of 32, which may not match the best hardware-specific block size.
Generated code size and branch complexity can reduce instruction-cache efficiency and make compiler auto-optimization/debugging more difficult.
Expert knowledge. PLUTO is valuable when 2mm benefits from aggressive affine transformations beyond basic OpenMP. Its strongest reusable ideas are vector-friendly inner loops and legal fusion/reordering, but these should be retained only when profiling confirms that the extra schedule complexity beats a simpler tiled implementation.
3.3 Gemini
Observed optimization. Gemini preserves the original two-phase loop structure and adds one OpenMP parallel-for to each outer i loop, with j and k private. No loop collapse, tiling, explicit scheduling policy, or SIMD directive is added.
Advantages for speedup
The transformation is simple and low risk: each i row is independent, so outer-loop parallelization maps naturally to the kernel.
Low transformation complexity keeps loop-control overhead small and preserves compiler-friendly, readable code.
Using a parallel outer row loop gives each thread substantial work for medium/large matrices, which can amortize OpenMP scheduling cost.
The original numerical operation order inside each output element is preserved, reducing the chance of unexpected numerical differences from aggressive reordering.
Disadvantages / risks
Parallelizing only i may expose insufficient parallelism when NI is small relative to the thread count.
Two independent parallel-for directives create two separate parallel regions and therefore additional fork/join overhead.
No cache tiling is introduced, so the implementation misses a major optimization opportunity for the B/C access streams in large matrices.
No explicit SIMD/vectorization guidance is provided, leaving vectorization entirely to the downstream compiler.
Expert knowledge. Gemini provides a strong simplicity-first baseline. Its output is appropriate when thread-level parallelism alone is sufficient, but for high-performance 2mm the main missing knowledge is cache blocking and broader work sharing. A useful refinement would preserve the simple structure while adding tiling and/or collapse only when needed.
3.4 GPT-5.2
Observed optimization. GPT-5.2 creates a single OpenMP parallel region and uses two work-sharing loops with collapse(2) and schedule(static), parallelizing the i–j output space for both multiplication stages while keeping k private.
Advantages for speedup
A single parallel region can reduce repeated OpenMP team creation/destruction relative to two independent parallel-for regions.
collapse(2) exposes NI×NJ (and NI×NL) tasks instead of only NI tasks, improving thread utilization when the number of rows is not large enough by itself.
schedule(static) is a good fit for regular dense matrix multiplication because iterations have nearly uniform work, minimizing scheduler overhead.
The code remains much simpler than polyhedral tiled output, giving a favorable balance between parallelism and maintainability.
Disadvantages / risks
collapse(2) increases work-sharing granularity but does not fix memory locality in the reduction dimension; cache behavior of B and C remains a bottleneck.
For very large NI, outer-i parallelism may already provide enough work, so collapse(2) may add bookkeeping without meaningful scalability benefit.
No cache tiling or explicit vectorization is introduced, leaving significant matrix-multiplication optimization potential unused.
The implicit barrier between the two omp-for loops is required by the tmp dependency; although correct, it means the two stages cannot freely overlap.
Expert knowledge. GPT-5.2 demonstrates a useful OpenMP optimization rule for 2mm: reuse one parallel region and statically distribute the full output space when outer-loop parallelism is insufficient. For maximum speed, this strategy should be combined with cache blocking and compiler/SIMD-friendly inner loops rather than used alone.
4. Cross-Approach Knowledge for 2mm
Approach	Thread parallelism	Tiling/locality	Vectorization	Primary concern
PPCG	Outer i / tile blocks	Yes in tiled version (32)	Implicit/compiler-dependent	Fixed tile size; two parallel regions
PLUTO	Polyhedral OpenMP schedule	Yes in tiled version (32)	Explicit vector directives	High schedule/control complexity
Gemini	Outer i only	No	Compiler-dependent	Limited parallelism/locality optimization
GPT-5.2	collapse(2), static, one team	No	Compiler-dependent	Good work sharing but misses cache blocking
5. Recommended Knowledge Rules for Optimizing 2mm
Prioritize cache blocking because 2mm is dominated by two dense matrix multiplications; thread parallelism alone does not address the main locality bottleneck.
Parallelize independent output rows/tiles. Use collapse(2) when NI alone does not provide enough parallel work, but avoid unnecessary collapse when row-level parallelism already saturates the machine.
Prefer one persistent OpenMP parallel region across the two dependent phases when practical, with a barrier between phases, to reduce repeated fork/join overhead.
Use static scheduling for the regular, balanced iteration space unless measurements show load imbalance.
Choose tile sizes according to cache capacity, data type, matrix dimensions, and thread count; treat 32 as a candidate rather than a universal constant.
Keep the innermost loops simple and vectorizable. Favor contiguous accesses and explicit SIMD/vector guidance when the compiler needs help.
Profile aggressive polyhedral fusion/reordering against a simpler tiled OpenMP baseline; legal transformations are not automatically faster on every architecture.
Validate numerical equivalence after transformations, especially if future optimizations alter floating-point reduction order.
Expert Knowledge Documentation: PolyBench 3mm
Speedup-oriented comparison of PPCG, PLUTO, Gemini, and GPT-5.2
This document is derived from the supplied source-code variants. Performance advantages/disadvantages are expert expectations based on the observed transformations; they are not measured runtime claims.
1. Source Variants Inspected
PPCG: 3mm_ppcg_openmp.c and 3mm_ppcg_tile.c
PLUTO: 3mm.pluto.c and 3mm.pluto.tile.c
Gemini: linear-algebra/kernels/3mm/3mm.c
GPT-5.2: linear-algebra/kernels/3mm/3mm.c
2. Application Optimization Characteristics
Three dense matrix multiplications are executed in sequence: E=A×B, F=C×D, and G=E×F. E and F are independent of one another, while G depends on both. The kernels have high arithmetic intensity and regular memory access, making loop blocking, SIMD/vectorization, and coarse-grained thread parallelism particularly relevant.
3. Approach-by-Approach Knowledge
Approach	Observed optimization	Speedup advantages	Speedup disadvantages
PPCG – OpenMP	Generates three separate OpenMP-parallel matrix-multiplication nests, parallelizing the outer output-row dimension.	Simple, regular work partitioning; low transformation complexity; good coarse-grained parallelism for large matrices; preserves straightforward matrix-multiplication structure.	Each multiplication enters a separate parallel region; no cache blocking; no explicit SIMD directives; E and F are still executed sequentially even though they are independent.
PPCG – Tiled	Applies 32×32×32 blocking to all three products and parallelizes outer tiles.	Improves cache reuse for A/B, C/D, and E/F; reduces working-set pressure; exposes coarse tile-level parallelism; particularly suitable for dense matrix multiplication.	Fixed tile size may not match every cache/matrix size; additional loop-control overhead and edge handling; three products are still globally sequential.
PLUTO – Original	Uses affine schedule transformations, OpenMP parallel loops, and explicit vectorization hints (ivdep/vector always) on suitable inner loops.	Combines thread-level parallelism with SIMD opportunities; transformed scheduling can improve locality and expose parallel dimensions beyond the original source order.	Generated control flow is substantially more complex; repeated parallel regions can add overhead; locality is less explicit than in the tiled version; transformed bounds may inhibit compiler optimization on some systems.
PLUTO – Tiled	Adds 32-sized multidimensional tiles to the affine schedule and retains OpenMP plus vectorization directives.	Strong combination of temporal/spatial locality, SIMD, and thread parallelism; blocking benefits all three matrix products; can reduce cache misses compared with untiled forms.	Very complex generated loop bounds and cleanup cases; fixed blocking may be suboptimal; code-size/control overhead can be significant; still does not explicitly overlap E and F.
Gemini	Adds one omp parallel for around the outer i loop of each of the three matrix multiplications.	Low-risk transformation; large independent row chunks; little scheduling complexity; easy for the compiler to optimize further.	Creates three distinct parallel regions; no collapse, tiling, SIMD, or explicit scheduling; does not exploit the independence of E and F; cache reuse remains essentially baseline.
GPT-5.2	Uses one omp parallel region containing three omp for collapse(2) phases; computes each output with a private scalar sum.	Reuses the same thread team across all three products, reducing fork/join overhead; collapse(2) exposes more tasks when one matrix dimension is small; scalar accumulation is optimizer-friendly.	Implicit barriers remain between phases; no cache blocking or SIMD directives; collapse(2) can make scheduling less cache-friendly; E and F are not computed concurrently.
4. Cross-Approach Synthesis
The compiler-generated variants emphasize dependence-aware affine transformations and, in their tiled versions, explicit locality optimization. The LLM-generated variants generally favor simpler OpenMP edits that preserve the original program structure. This simplicity can reduce transformation overhead and improve maintainability, while PPCG/PLUTO can expose locality and legal parallelism that are difficult to derive from a local pragma insertion alone.
A strong SmartCompiler-style strategy should therefore combine the two: retain compiler legality/dependence knowledge for tiling and scheduling, but prefer the simplest parallel structure that achieves the required locality and thread utilization on the target machine.
5. Reusable Expert Knowledge for Speedup
Prioritize cache blocking for all three matrix products; a tile size around 32 is a reasonable starting point but should be tuned to cache size and data type.
Use one persistent OpenMP parallel region when possible to avoid repeatedly creating thread teams across E, F, and G.
Preserve the dependency barrier before G, but E and F may be computed concurrently using OpenMP sections/tasks when resources and matrix sizes justify it.
Keep the reduction/accumulation dimension innermost or otherwise structured to maximize vectorization and reuse.
Combine thread-level parallelism with SIMD rather than relying on OpenMP outer-loop parallelism alone.
For small problem sizes, avoid excessive collapse, tasking, or many parallel regions because runtime overhead can dominate.
6. Knowledge Guidance for SmartCompiler
When optimizing this application, use the rules above as constraints and preferences rather than blindly copying any one generated version. Select transformations according to matrix/grid size, thread count, cache hierarchy, vector width, and measured overhead. Favor a hybrid candidate that preserves compiler-proven legality while reducing unnecessary parallel-region creation, complex cleanup logic, and fine-grained scheduling.
Expert Knowledge Documentation: PolyBench Cholesky
Speedup-oriented comparison of PPCG, PLUTO, Gemini, and GPT-5.2
This document is derived from the supplied source-code variants. Performance advantages/disadvantages are expert expectations based on the observed transformations; they are not measured runtime claims.
1. Source Variants Inspected
PPCG: cholesky_ppcg_openmp.c and cholesky_ppcg_tile.c
PLUTO: cholesky.pluto.c and cholesky.pluto.tile.c
Gemini: linear-algebra/solvers/cholesky/cholesky.c
GPT-5.2: linear-algebra/solvers/cholesky/cholesky.c
2. Application Optimization Characteristics
Cholesky factorization has strong loop-carried dependencies: each diagonal element and column depends on previously computed columns. Parallelism therefore lies mainly in updating independent rows/blocks after a pivot/column becomes available. Unlike GEMM, blindly parallelizing inner reductions or dependent outer loops can be ineffective or incorrect.
3. Approach-by-Approach Knowledge
Approach	Observed optimization	Speedup advantages	Speedup disadvantages
PPCG – OpenMP	Keeps the dependent outer factorization sequential and introduces omp parallel for within update work after a diagonal/pivot step.	Respects major dependencies; exposes parallel work only where the transformed dependence analysis permits it; useful as trailing work grows.	Parallel regions occur inside sequential outer progress and may be too fine-grained; repeated fork/join overhead can dominate; no explicit vectorization; early iterations have little parallel work.
PPCG – Tiled	Applies blocking (primarily 32-sized regions) to dependent factorization/update phases and adds OpenMP to selected block updates.	Blocking can improve locality and increase the amount of work per parallel update; tile structure can better expose trailing-submatrix concurrency.	Generated code is highly complex with many boundary/special cases; fixed tile size is architecture/problem-size sensitive; some parallel loops remain fine-grained; synchronization/dependency overhead is unavoidable.
PLUTO – Original	Uses affine rescheduling and OpenMP over legal transformed update dimensions while preserving sequential dependence phases.	Can expose parallel update fronts not obvious in the source; coarse transformed iterations may amortize some overhead; dependence legality is compiler-derived.	Complex schedule and bounds; available parallelism varies over factorization progress; no clear cache blocking in original; synchronization may limit scalability.
PLUTO – Tiled	Introduces block/tile scheduling (including 16/32-scale bounds in generated code) and OpenMP on legal block updates.	Improves reuse of previously computed factors and creates larger block updates; more suitable for cache hierarchy and multicore execution than scalar updates.	Very complicated generated control flow; nonuniform triangular domains can load-imbalance threads; tile choice must balance dependency latency, cache reuse, and available concurrency.
Gemini	Reorders around column j: computes the diagonal sequentially, then parallelizes independent i>j row updates with omp parallel for.	A natural and safe Cholesky parallelization: all rows below the current diagonal can be updated independently once A[j][j] is known; simple code and meaningful coarse work for later/larger columns.	A new parallel worksharing operation occurs for every column; early/late columns may have insufficient work; inner dot products are sequential and not explicitly vectorized or blocked.
GPT-5.2	For each i/j, allocates temporary arrays, parallelizes elementwise product generation, then performs the subtraction sequentially; repeats allocation/free operations inside the factorization.	Avoids a data race on scalar accumulation by parallelizing independent product formation; static scheduling is predictable; mathematically preserves the sequential reduction order in a second pass.	Frequent dynamic allocation/free inside nested loops is expensive; parallel regions target short k loops and can cost more than they save; doubles memory traffic through temporary buffers; does not exploit the stronger row-level parallelism used by Gemini; likely poor scalability for typical PolyBench sizes.
4. Cross-Approach Synthesis
The compiler-generated variants emphasize dependence-aware affine transformations and, in their tiled versions, explicit locality optimization. The LLM-generated variants generally favor simpler OpenMP edits that preserve the original program structure. This simplicity can reduce transformation overhead and improve maintainability, while PPCG/PLUTO can expose locality and legal parallelism that are difficult to derive from a local pragma insertion alone.
A strong SmartCompiler-style strategy should therefore combine the two: retain compiler legality/dependence knowledge for tiling and scheduling, but prefer the simplest parallel structure that achieves the required locality and thread utilization on the target machine.
5. Reusable Expert Knowledge for Speedup
Do not parallelize the dependent outer factorization dimension without a blocked/task dependency scheme.
After a diagonal/column is ready, parallelize independent row or trailing-block updates rather than short inner dot-product loops.
Use blocked Cholesky to convert scalar updates into larger matrix operations with better cache reuse and more parallel work.
Avoid allocating temporary arrays inside hot nested loops; use thread-local scalar/vector reductions or blocked kernels instead.
Exploit SIMD for dot products while keeping the reduction local to a thread.
Use tasks with dependencies or coarse block-level OpenMP only when block sizes provide enough work to amortize scheduling and synchronization.
6. Knowledge Guidance for SmartCompiler
When optimizing this application, use the rules above as constraints and preferences rather than blindly copying any one generated version. Select transformations according to matrix/grid size, thread count, cache hierarchy, vector width, and measured overhead. Favor a hybrid candidate that preserves compiler-proven legality while reducing unnecessary parallel-region creation, complex cleanup logic, and fine-grained scheduling.
Expert Knowledge Documentation: PolyBench GEMM
Speedup-oriented comparison of PPCG, PLUTO, Gemini, and GPT-5.2
This document is derived from the supplied source-code variants. Performance advantages/disadvantages are expert expectations based on the observed transformations; they are not measured runtime claims.
1. Source Variants Inspected
PPCG: gemm_ppcg_openmp.c and gemm_ppcg_tile.c
PLUTO: gemm.pluto.c and gemm.pluto.tile.c
Gemini: linear-algebra/blas/gemm/gemm.c
GPT-5.2: linear-algebra/blas/gemm/gemm.c
2. Application Optimization Characteristics
GEMM computes C = beta·C + alpha·A·B. It is compute-dense, highly regular, and a classic target for hierarchical blocking, vectorization, register reuse, and thread parallelism. The main optimization objective is to maximize reuse of A and B while writing C efficiently.
3. Approach-by-Approach Knowledge
Approach	Observed optimization	Speedup advantages	Speedup disadvantages
